Fillets: a concave rounding along an acute corner whose edge turns towards
the ball, walked along the balls' centres (mitcad#1396).

The case: an end wall standing at 45 degrees to a rail on a plate, the
rail's foot rounded with 2 and its top with 1.2; the wall's foot along the
plate and the rail's profile rounded with 1.5. A walk takes the ball whose
contacts' mid-point M lies in the guide's normal plane at the guide's
parameter; the guide is the spine (the edges on the wall). In the acute
corner the ball lies far from the edge (2.6 radii at 45 degrees), and where
the edge turns round the foot rounding (an ellipse, its radius of curvature
1.41 at the rail's side) the mid-point lay 3.1 from the guide along its
principal normal: k (M - G).n was 2.2, above 1, where the normal planes turn
back faster than the ball moves. The walk from the analytic piece along the
rail's side found its next section on the foot rounding's cylinder beyond
the face, every step, and failed at once ("the walk between the two faces
fails"; from radius 1 up: at 0.5 the fold is 0.73 and the walk went
through).

- ChFi3d_UnfoldGuide (new, ChFi3d_Builder_Guide.cxx), called by
  PerformSetOfKPart after ChFi3d_PerformElSpine for each guide: the guide of
  a constant-radius rounding's walk between two pieces (its Previous and
  Next) whose spine folds, k (M - G).n of 0.9 or more somewhere (M taken
  between the end balls' mid-points along the way), becomes the cubic from
  the centre of the ball the walk starts on (the previous piece's last
  section, read from its contacts and its faces' normals) to the centre of
  the ball it ends on (the next piece's first), with the spine's tangents
  there and a circle's handles. Each end ball's centre and mid-point lie in
  the spine's normal plane at its end (else it is left), so the walk starts
  and ends on the same sections; between them the guide runs near the
  balls' centres and its normal planes turn with the ball. Taken only where
  the cubic itself folds less than 0.5. The blend is the same rolling ball:
  the guide says which ball each section is.

On the reported design's stored body before the item (both sides of a
mirror, 14 edges): valid, the result and the stored body within 0.0046 mm
of each other along both chains (the stored foot faces' own fit; the
result's points within 0.001 mm of the stored body), no piece over 1e-4 mm3
in the boolean difference of the two; before, the fillet failed. On a
synthetic plate, rail and end wall (OCCT alone), at radii 1, 1.5 and 1.9:
valid, closed, every point of the walked face over the foot rounding within
1.1e-5 mm of the ball (R from the analytic path of its centre); before,
each failed.

Upstream: new.
